course: dictionary
lesson: 4179
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «Gold» can name a precious metal, a colour, or a prize. The words around it give the clue.
explain: «Spices were dear in medieval Europe but not usually worth as much as gold.» The words worth and dear tell you that gold is the precious metal that costs a lot. Test it: swap in "a shiny yellow metal" and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: «Kintsugi is a Japanese art of mending broken pottery with gold.» Mending pottery is something you do with a material, so gold here is the metal. «A gold medal» is a prize for first place, and «golden» can mean very good, as in «a golden chance».
fact: Gold is a soft, shiny yellow metal that is rare and costs a lot of money.
fact: Gold is also a bright yellow colour.
fact: A gold medal is the prize for first place; silver is for second and bronze is for third.
fact: Golden means made of gold or the colour of gold, and it can also mean very good, as in «a golden chance».
fact: Gold is an element, and it does not rust.
exercise: «Spices were dear in medieval Europe but not usually worth as much as gold.» In this sentence, gold means | a golden chance | a colour of a wall | *a precious yellow metal | a medal for third place
exercise: Ella says, «She won gold in the race!» What does gold mean here? | *A first-place prize | A bronze medal for third place | A pot of gold at the end of a rainbow | A soft, dull grey metal
exercise: «In Australia the $1 and $2 coins are gold-coloured, so people call them "gold coins", though they contain no gold.» What does this show? | The coins are made of pure gold | Gold coins are always worth a lot | *Gold can describe a colour, even when there is no real gold | Australian coins are never yellow
test: «Dad's car is a golden colour.» What does golden mean here? | *Yellow, like gold | Very good | Cold | Made of solid gold
test: A prize for first place is a ___ medal. | Silver | *Gold | Tin | Bronze
exam: Which sentence uses gold to mean a prize? | The goose laid golden eggs. | The sand was pale gold in the sun. | Pirates hid a chest of gold in the cave. | *The swimmer won gold at the Games.
